dule in august  lowry felt  two lumps under her arm after three  months of delayed screenings and tests  she finally got her diagnosis stage  three breast cancer  it's been a little emotional  but i just keep my faith and trust in  god  if her cancer had been detected last may  or june doctors believe  they may have been able to prevent the  spread last week lowery had  a double mastectomy they put our life  on a standstill what was the hardest  part for you  the hardest part was telling my kids  i didn't want them to worry  i didn't want to be crying experts  predict the number of people who will  die from breast or colorectal cancer in  the u.s  will increase by nearly 10 000 over the  next decade because of covid19's impact  on cancer care at some point  dr karen finkelstein who treats  gynecologic cancers  has seen a 30 percent decline in cancer  referrals  i'm being paralyzed i've been frozen by  kovid because  it's made it's changed the landscape of  of medicine and patients  last year has taken a toll on her  personally you start to  feel a sense of despair the hurdles  kovid has  forced us to face this year some of them  have been insurmountable i love you it's  that love from john stone's family and  for her family that gives her the  strength to keep fighting  it doesn't seem like you're going to  take this sitting down are you  no i'm not i'm going to get it  i'm going to get it good for cbs this  morning  medela villarreal dallas  we want her to get it good too our dr  david agus who specializes in treating  patients with advanced cancers  joins us now good morning to you david  this is such a great wake-up call to  those of us who have put off screenings  for whatever reason  so here's a question what screening  should we absolutely not put off or  delay  well you know during cover there was  almost a 50 drop in the diagnosis of  breast cancer week by week and so  those breast cancers are still there  they're going to be diagnosed so you  need to get your mammogram  you need to get a psa test if you're man  a lung screening test if you're a smoker  colonoscopy all the standard screening  tests and you need to go to your doctor  to get that standard blood test  other things are found in the blood test  hospitals have remarkable programs now  to prevent and most doctors are actually  and  nurses are vaccinated so it is very low  risk to go  get the screening you know and then some  vaccinated  people are reporting swollen lymph nodes  underneath their armpits which can  sometimes be mistaken  as a sign of breast cancer what do you  have to say about that  yeah gail i mean you're right is that  after the vaccination for six to eight  weeks lymphos get enlarged and that's  part of your  your immune response and they're  actually lymph nodes in the breast so  you can get false positives if you have  a mammogram  or other medical test during that time  period so if you can if they're elective  if they're part of your routine  screening  delay at six to eight weeks after the  vaccine  can i talk about the astrazeneca the  latest thing that we're hearing there  that now they're saying the efficacy is  now 76  down from 79 percent it's very slight i  get it but should people be concerned it  seems every day there's something new  about astrazeneca are you worried  yeah i'm worried about the  communications group i mean this was a a  problem of omission they put the the  data they had in february  but february to march there were another  49 cases and the data went from 79  to 76 but the data in the elderly went  from 80 to 85 percent so these are small  changes  it is still an amazing vaccine and we  shouldn't let this noise  affect our acceptance of something that  could save our lives all right dr david  agus thank you so much  we'll be right back

